/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each satellite carries atomic clocks (rubidium and caesium), stable to about one part in 10¹³ over a day.
   - δts is the satellite clock offset in s; af0 is the offset (s), af1 the drift (s/s) and af2 the drift rate (s/s²) at the reference time toc.
   - Relativity also moves the clock: weaker gravity makes it run fast and orbital speed makes it run slow, a net gain of about 38 µs a day, or 11.4 km of range.
   - Orbit (ephemeris) error is different: it is not subtracted from the range.
   - The ionosphere holds free electrons from about 60 km to 1000 km.
   - dI is the extra range in metres; TEC in electrons/m²; f is the carrier frequency in Hz.
   - Because dI depends on frequency, two frequencies remove it.
   - The troposphere is the neutral air below the tropopause (about 9 km over the poles, 16 to 17 km over the tropics).
   - Multipath (late reflected copies) adds about 1 to 5 m; receiver noise is smaller.
*/
